1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,023
November fourteenth, two thousand four.

2
00:00:03,023 --> 00:00:11,163
A hundred miles off San Diego, four Navy aviators look down at the Pacific and see something that should not be there.

3
00:00:11,163 --> 00:00:17,233
A smooth white shape, like a giant Tic Tac, with no wings, hovering over churning water.

4
00:00:17,699 --> 00:00:24,292
For days, a warship's radar had been tracking strange contacts in this same patch of sky.

5
00:00:24,292 --> 00:00:28,588
That afternoon, an infrared camera would film one of them.

6
00:00:28,588 --> 00:00:31,700
Radar, eyes and a camera, all on one case.

7
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:38,733
For three thousand years, every mystery in the sky reached us as a story told by a witness.

8
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:41,114
Ancient chroniclers had no radar.

9
00:00:41,114 --> 00:00:44,866
At Nimitz, for once, the machines were watching too.

10
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:49,930
So tonight we follow the evidence down the chain, link by link.

11
00:00:49,930 --> 00:00:50,368
Radar.

12
00:00:50,368 --> 00:00:50,733
Eyes.

13
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:51,243
Camera.

14
00:00:51,243 --> 00:00:51,608
Math.

15
00:00:51,608 --> 00:00:53,213
And the missing tapes.

16
00:00:53,213 --> 00:00:57,592
Each link moves our meter, from sensor glitch to real craft.

17
00:00:57,592 --> 00:01:00,000
The full scorecard is at the end.

18
00:01:04,466 --> 00:01:05,892
Link one: the radar.

19
00:01:05,892 --> 00:01:14,806
The ship is the USS Princeton, a guided missile cruiser escorting the carrier Nimitz during training off Southern California.

20
00:01:14,806 --> 00:01:18,799
Its SPY-1 radar is among the most powerful in the fleet.

21
00:01:19,266 --> 00:01:24,755
The strike group was in its final exam before deploying to the Middle East.

22
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:32,000
Around November tenth, radar operator Kevin Day began seeing odd contacts near San Clemente Island.

23
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:41,047
Groups of five to ten objects at about twenty-eight thousand feet, drifting south at roughly a hundred and twenty knots.

24
00:01:41,047 --> 00:01:42,406
Too high for birds.

25
00:01:42,406 --> 00:01:43,693
Too slow for jets.

26
00:01:43,693 --> 00:01:45,266
And on no flight plan.

27
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:56,166
According to the crew's later accounts, some contacts first appeared extremely high, around eighty thousand feet, and then dropped toward the ocean in seconds.

28
00:01:56,633 --> 00:01:59,212
The crew suspected the radar itself.

29
00:01:59,212 --> 00:02:00,646
Ghost tracks happen.

30
00:02:00,646 --> 00:02:05,518
So, by their account, they took the system down and recalibrated it.

31
00:02:05,518 --> 00:02:07,166
The contacts came back.

32
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:11,803
This is the strongest argument against a simple glitch.

33
00:02:11,803 --> 00:02:13,167
One radar can lie.

34
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:20,066
But crew members say other sensors, including an E-2 Hawkeye radar plane, saw contacts too.

35
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:22,047
But notice the problem.

36
00:02:22,047 --> 00:02:28,237
Almost everything we know about these tracks comes from what operators remembered years later.

37
00:02:28,237 --> 00:02:31,266
The raw radar data has never been made public.

38
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:33,186
Reading for link one.

39
00:02:33,186 --> 00:02:37,754
Something was probably on the scopes, and crews took it seriously.

40
00:02:37,754 --> 00:02:40,800
What it was, the radar alone cannot tell us.

41
00:02:41,266 --> 00:02:42,633
Link two: the eyes.

42
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:52,199
On November fourteenth, two F/A-18F Super Hornets are pulled off a training exercise and sent toward one of the Princeton's contacts.

43
00:02:52,666 --> 00:02:58,957
In the lead jet, Commander David Fravor, commanding officer of the Black Aces squadron.

44
00:02:58,957 --> 00:03:01,849
In the second, Lieutenant Alex Dietrich.

45
00:03:01,849 --> 00:03:05,536
Each jet has a weapons systems officer in the back.

46
00:03:05,536 --> 00:03:07,199
Four trained observers.

47
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:11,089
The controller says they are merging with the target.

48
00:03:11,089 --> 00:03:20,066
They look down and see a patch of churning white water, roughly the size of an airliner's body, as if something lay just under the surface.

49
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:24,164
Above it, a white object, about forty feet long.

50
00:03:24,164 --> 00:03:24,694
Smooth.

51
00:03:24,694 --> 00:03:27,115
No wings, no rotors, no exhaust.

52
00:03:27,115 --> 00:03:32,033
It moves back and forth over the water, with no predictable path.

53
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:35,547
Fravor spirals down to get a closer look.

54
00:03:35,547 --> 00:03:39,115
He says the object rises and mirrors his circle.

55
00:03:39,115 --> 00:03:43,129
When he cuts across to intercept, it accelerates away.

56
00:03:43,129 --> 00:03:45,433
In his words, it was just gone.

57
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:48,609
Seconds later the Princeton calls again.

58
00:03:48,609 --> 00:03:55,384
A contact has appeared at the pilots' secret meeting point, their CAP point, about sixty miles away.

59
00:03:55,384 --> 00:03:58,433
The jets are low on fuel, and they head home.

60
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:00,900
Now the inconvenient detail.

61
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:05,185
Fravor describes watching the object for about five minutes.

62
00:04:05,185 --> 00:04:10,685
Dietrich has said she was visual for roughly ten seconds of hard maneuvering.

63
00:04:10,685 --> 00:04:13,400
Same event, two very different clocks.

64
00:04:13,866 --> 00:04:16,181
Dietrich is careful about memory.

65
00:04:16,181 --> 00:04:18,636
She wrote her account within hours.

66
00:04:18,636 --> 00:04:25,300
But she says she does not identify as a UFO person, and she will not claim to know what it was.

67
00:04:25,766 --> 00:04:27,538
Fravor is less cautious.

68
00:04:27,538 --> 00:04:37,133
Testifying to Congress under oath in twenty twenty-three, he said the technology they faced was far superior to anything they had.

69
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,112
Reading for link two.

70
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:44,080
The eyes give us the most vivid evidence, and the hardest to measure.

71
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:46,672
Four aviators saw something strange.

72
00:04:46,672 --> 00:04:50,200
Nobody could measure its distance, size or speed.

73
00:04:50,666 --> 00:04:52,340
Link three: the camera.

74
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,543
About an hour later, a second wave launches.

75
00:04:55,543 --> 00:05:05,733
In one jet, Lieutenant Commander Chad Underwood operates an ATFLIR pod, an infrared targeting camera built to lock on to targets miles away.

76
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:10,329
He finds a contact on radar and slews the camera onto it.

77
00:05:10,329 --> 00:05:13,734
On the screen: a small, featureless white oval.

78
00:05:13,734 --> 00:05:16,197
Underwood is the one who named it.

79
00:05:16,197 --> 00:05:18,733
It looked, he said, like a Tic Tac.

80
00:05:19,199 --> 00:05:21,692
But he never saw it with his own eyes.

81
00:05:21,692 --> 00:05:23,004
It was too far away.

82
00:05:23,004 --> 00:05:29,366
He concentrated on keeping the tape running, so the intelligence team on the ship could study it.

83
00:05:29,833 --> 00:05:32,829
That tape became the clip known as FLIR1.

84
00:05:32,829 --> 00:05:36,995
Its audio did not survive on the copy that left the ship.

85
00:05:36,995 --> 00:05:41,599
A version was already circulating online by two thousand seven.

86
00:05:42,066 --> 00:05:46,075
The New York Times published it in December twenty seventeen.

87
00:05:46,075 --> 00:05:53,566
Two years later the Navy confirmed it was genuine, and in April twenty twenty the Pentagon officially released it.

88
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:56,411
The famous moment comes at the end.

89
00:05:56,411 --> 00:06:01,576
The object seems to dart off to the left, faster than the camera can follow.

90
00:06:01,576 --> 00:06:04,566
For many viewers, that is the craft leaving.

91
00:06:05,033 --> 00:06:08,500
Analyst Mick West reads those seconds differently.

92
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:15,433
Just before the dart, the camera changes its field of view and zoom, and the tracker loses its lock.

93
00:06:15,899 --> 00:06:22,468
Once the lock is gone, the camera stops following, and a slow drift can look like a sudden jump.

94
00:06:22,468 --> 00:06:26,300
To West, the clip is consistent with a distant aircraft.

95
00:06:26,766 --> 00:06:28,297
Underwood pushes back.

96
00:06:28,297 --> 00:06:34,557
His jet was flying straight and level, he says, so his maneuvering did not cause the jump.

97
00:06:34,557 --> 00:06:39,566
But he also admits he cannot confirm that the object really accelerated.

98
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:41,744
Then there is the heat.

99
00:06:41,744 --> 00:06:43,605
No visible exhaust plume.

100
00:06:43,605 --> 00:06:45,465
Believers say: no engine.

101
00:06:45,465 --> 00:06:52,833
Skeptics say: at long range, a jet's hot engines can blur into exactly this kind of shapeless glow.

102
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,996
Reading for link three.

103
00:06:54,996 --> 00:07:00,084
The video is genuine Navy footage, and officially still unidentified.

104
00:07:00,084 --> 00:07:03,255
But it shows a blob at an unknown distance.

105
00:07:03,255 --> 00:07:06,500
On its own, it proves nothing extraordinary.

106
00:07:06,966 --> 00:07:08,316
Link four: the math.

107
00:07:08,316 --> 00:07:12,230
If the witnesses are right, how hard did this object move?

108
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:20,733
In twenty nineteen, physicist Kevin Knuth and two colleagues tried to put numbers on it, in the peer reviewed journal Entropy.

109
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:31,633
They started from Kevin Day's account of a contact dropping from twenty-eight thousand feet to about fifty feet above the sea in seventy-eight hundredths of a second.

110
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:38,416
Their most probable estimate: more than five thousand g, reaching around Mach sixty.

111
00:07:38,416 --> 00:07:41,800
Fighter pilots train to survive about nine g.

112
00:07:42,266 --> 00:07:49,746
They estimated the power needed at more than ten times the entire nuclear output of the United States.

113
00:07:49,746 --> 00:07:53,633
Yet no one reported a sonic boom, or a flash of heat.

114
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:56,607
There is a second, older analysis.

115
00:07:56,607 --> 00:08:03,171
In twenty ten, a contractor working for the Defense Intelligence Agency studied the case.

116
00:08:03,171 --> 00:08:10,399
In October twenty twenty-six, the Pentagon included that report in its official UFO file releases.

117
00:08:10,866 --> 00:08:16,830
Its engineers tried a range of assumed times for a fall from sixty thousand feet.

118
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:20,733
Five seconds gives about Mach ten and seventy-five g.

119
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:23,236
Longer falls give smaller numbers.

120
00:08:23,236 --> 00:08:27,433
And the report admits it had relatively little hard data.

121
00:08:27,899 --> 00:08:29,120
Here is the catch.

122
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:31,902
The math is only as strong as its inputs.

123
00:08:31,902 --> 00:08:37,600
Change seventy-eight hundredths of a second to ten seconds, and the miracle shrinks.

124
00:08:38,066 --> 00:08:42,190
To its credit, Knuth's own paper spells out the options.

125
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:50,733
These observations are either fabricated, seriously in error, or show technology far beyond anything known on Earth.

126
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:52,835
Reading for link four.

127
00:08:52,835 --> 00:08:56,625
Impressive arithmetic, built on remembered numbers.

128
00:08:56,625 --> 00:08:58,706
It does not decide the case.

129
00:08:58,706 --> 00:09:02,199
It shows how much rides on data we do not have.

130
00:09:02,666 --> 00:09:04,709
Link five: the missing tapes.

131
00:09:04,709 --> 00:09:08,441
If the radar data existed, why not simply release it?

132
00:09:08,441 --> 00:09:12,033
Here the case turns into a mystery about paperwork.

133
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:21,767
Several crew members later said that people arrived after the encounter and collected recordings, from the Hawkeye and from the radar.

134
00:09:21,767 --> 00:09:24,533
These are testimonies, still unverified.

135
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:34,799
In twenty twenty-five, Tim Phillips, a former acting head of the Pentagon's UFO office AARO, gave an official version of what happened next.

136
00:09:35,266 --> 00:09:43,699
Third Fleet ordered the radar tapes preserved, he said, and they were sent to a Navy lab in Dahlgren, Virginia, for analysis.

137
00:09:43,699 --> 00:09:46,533
Two physical searches could not find them.

138
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,881
Even the ships' operational journals for that period could not be found.

139
00:09:51,881 --> 00:09:57,033
Phillips said he was not sure if that was intentional, or just incompetence.

140
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,344
And the mysterious visitors?

141
00:09:59,344 --> 00:10:07,906
Phillips said AARO traced them to DARPA personnel working with the Navy on a new surveillance system, some wearing dark jumpsuits.

142
00:10:07,906 --> 00:10:11,133
AARO could not find the flight log to confirm it.

143
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:16,849
AARO's first director, Sean Kirkpatrick, put it bluntly in twenty twenty-four.

144
00:10:16,849 --> 00:10:17,993
There is no data.

145
00:10:17,993 --> 00:10:22,166
Not enough information, he said, to ever come to a conclusion.

146
00:10:22,633 --> 00:10:24,127
Reading for link five.

147
00:10:24,127 --> 00:10:27,728
The best evidence in this case may have existed once.

148
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:28,883
Today it is gone.

149
00:10:28,883 --> 00:10:33,366
And without it, every number we heard tonight is a reconstruction.

150
00:10:33,833 --> 00:10:34,841
So, who did it?

151
00:10:34,841 --> 00:10:37,531
Five explanations have been put forward.

152
00:10:37,531 --> 00:10:41,633
Let us line them up and test each one against all five links.

153
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:44,272
Suspect one: a sensor glitch.

154
00:10:44,272 --> 00:10:47,492
Radar ghosts and camera artifacts are real.

155
00:10:47,492 --> 00:10:54,533
But a glitch does not explain four aviators seeing the same white object over disturbed water.

156
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,296
Suspect two: a distant aircraft.

157
00:10:57,296 --> 00:10:59,808
It fits the camera clip quite well.

158
00:10:59,808 --> 00:11:06,266
It does not fit the close encounter that Fravor and Dietrich describe, low over the ocean.

159
00:11:06,733 --> 00:11:09,563
Suspect three: a drone or a test system.

160
00:11:09,563 --> 00:11:16,569
Phillips believes the Princeton may have tracked a new surveillance system flying at the wave tops.

161
00:11:16,569 --> 00:11:19,328
That could explain some radar contacts.

162
00:11:19,328 --> 00:11:22,866
Not a forty foot white object outflying a fighter.

163
00:11:23,333 --> 00:11:27,277
Suspect four: a secret program, American or foreign.

164
00:11:27,277 --> 00:11:33,799
Underwood says nobody ever gave him the usual quiet briefing that a test project gets.

165
00:11:34,266 --> 00:11:42,803
And a former AARO chief said that for some unexplained cases, he knew of no fielded system that could fly that way.

166
00:11:42,803 --> 00:11:44,733
Not proven, not ruled out.

167
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:47,428
Suspect five: a non-human craft.

168
00:11:47,428 --> 00:11:52,094
It would explain the performance claims, if those claims are right.

169
00:11:52,094 --> 00:12:00,033
But it is the only suspect that needs every uncertain number to be exactly correct, and it left no physical trace.

170
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,085
The answer may not be one suspect.

171
00:12:03,085 --> 00:12:09,775
The Pentagon's twenty twenty-one assessment said UAP probably lack a single explanation.

172
00:12:09,775 --> 00:12:16,466
A drone on radar, a jet on camera, and something stranger seen by eye could all be true.

173
00:12:16,933 --> 00:12:19,863
That is how old sky mysteries worked too.

174
00:12:19,863 --> 00:12:23,936
A chronicler saw fire in the heavens and wrote one story.

175
00:12:23,936 --> 00:12:27,437
Modern sensors were supposed to end that problem.

176
00:12:27,437 --> 00:12:29,366
At Nimitz, they almost did.

177
00:12:29,833 --> 00:12:30,598
Case closed.

178
00:12:30,598 --> 00:12:32,000
Here is the scorecard.

179
00:12:32,466 --> 00:12:38,291
The Princeton tracked unknown contacts for days: evidence, from several crew.

180
00:12:38,291 --> 00:12:43,284
Four aviators saw a wingless white object: evidence, as testimony.

181
00:12:43,284 --> 00:12:46,462
FLIR1 is authentic Navy footage: evidence.

182
00:12:46,462 --> 00:12:49,866
The video shows impossible motion: not shown.

183
00:12:50,333 --> 00:12:56,029
Five thousand g and Mach sixty: unverified, built on recalled numbers.

184
00:12:56,029 --> 00:12:58,714
The original radar data: missing.

185
00:12:58,714 --> 00:13:01,399
Proof of a non-human craft: none.

186
00:13:01,866 --> 00:13:02,708
The verdict.

187
00:13:02,708 --> 00:13:04,112
Not a simple glitch.

188
00:13:04,112 --> 00:13:07,341
Too many people and too many sensors for that.

189
00:13:07,341 --> 00:13:09,447
But not a proven craft either.

190
00:13:09,447 --> 00:13:16,466
The Nimitz Tic Tac is a real event with missing evidence, and that is exactly why it refuses to die.

191
00:13:16,933 --> 00:13:22,930
Our meter ends close to the middle, leaning toward a real object of unknown origin.

192
00:13:22,930 --> 00:13:28,854
If the Princeton's tapes ever surface, this case could be settled in an afternoon.

193
00:13:28,854 --> 00:13:30,733
Until then, it stays open.

194
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:32,693
That is the case file.

195
00:13:32,693 --> 00:13:35,748
Glitch, drone, secret jet, or something else?

196
00:13:35,748 --> 00:13:40,433
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next mystery on trial.
